{
 "id": "2021-schirpke-mountain",
 "name": "Mountain Lakes: Visual Preferences",
 "description": "Faithful replication of the photo-based online questionnaire in Schirpke, U., Scolozzi, R., & Tappeiner, U. (2021). \"A gem among the rocks\"—Identifying and measuring visual preferences for mountain lakes. Water 13(9):1151. https://doi.org/10.3390/w13091151\n\nAfter the paper's definition of a mountain lake (\"smaller-sized natural lakes, which are located at least 1000 meters above sea level\"), participants answer the open question \"During an excursion you pass by a natural mountain lake. What best defines the characteristics of this lake?\". They then see the paper's six picture sets one by one, with the images of each set in random order, and choose the image they prefer: (1) water clarity, 2 images; (2) water colour, 4; (3) presence of algae, 3; (4) lake shore at lower altitudes, 4; (5) lake shore at higher altitudes, 4; (6) surrounding land cover, 4. Last come optional questions on gender, age, first language, relation to the Alps, time spent in nature and lake visits per year. Image ids follow the paper's Figure 3 (set2_a = image 2a, …).\n\nOriginal study: 526 people reached through Alpine clubs and limnology networks in Austria, Germany and Italy (online, July–December 2020). Most preferred clear water (1a, 83.5%), blue water (2a/2b), no algae (3a), shrubs or grass with rocks at lower shores (4b/4d), large rocks at higher shores (5a, 70%) and forest around the lake (6d, 47%); preferences for the water were very similar across socio-cultural groups.\n\nAnalysis as in the paper: share of choices per image within each set, paired t-tests between images, and comparisons across the socio-cultural groups; code the open answers into lake characteristics.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) the images are cropped from the paper's Figure 3, so the top strip carrying the result labels is removed and each image is slightly smaller than in the questionnaire; (2) English only (the original also ran in German and Italian); (3) the wording of the socio-cultural questions is ours, built from the paper's Table 3 classes (e.g. lake affinity is asked as number of lake visits per year so the paper's split can be made); (4) the picture-set topics are not shown to participants.\n\nImages: Figure 3 of the open-access article (CC BY 4.0), shown with credit for research; the template is removed if the authors object.",
